#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055528} :activity/id #uuid "14506510-6a23-50e7-9f92-2d6a0e7b9372" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782 :topic/id #uuid "66aee53c-aecf-5378-b318-bdc0930f5e43" :topic/math-academy-id 140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055964 :tutorial/content "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$\\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}$\n- **Subtraction**\n$(f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3$\n- **Multiplication**\n$(fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2$\n- **Division**\n$(f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "c656d962-19ad-5e04-b117-fa8d0c9034b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2751 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10d1d758-8351-5c00-a262-8b57b04f8398" :step/math-academy-id 13680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054522 :question/id #uuid "9a523530-ec38-5df8-997f-2f94975eb87b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234198}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "725779d7-8771-596d-841c-0ca2dd5da761" :step/math-academy-id 13682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054523 :question/id #uuid "79c8f0a9-6f05-515f-abaa-553863402a06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234201}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c6da4ec9-5a2e-5037-ad6d-2b91d02a4fa0" :step/math-academy-id 13683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054524 :question/id #uuid "c68e70d0-ccec-578a-96c1-7ed106aebc79" :question/math-academy-id "e-3914"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aafd8db-c723-508f-9ea3-43881f0d9140" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234506}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "722ef25c-0cfd-5916-9719-a70b3e79029b" :step/math-academy-id 13684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054544 :question/id #uuid "b6a4d0a8-f579-5d6d-ad74-59b68d2046e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410421}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "d9de7d87-ac26-5915-a421-2a3c6f706e14" :step/math-academy-id 15087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055971 :tutorial/content "When we're asked to evaluate the sum of two functions at a particular point, there are two methods that we can use. We could either evaluate both functions at the point and sum the results. Or, we could sum the two functions and then evaluate the result at the point.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the $(f+g)(1)$ for\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x-3\n$$\n\nThe notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f + g)(1)$, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$, and then add the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (1)^{2} - 1 = 0 \\\\\ng(1) &= 2(1) - 3 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= f(1) + g(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(x) &= (x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f + g)(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= (1)^{2} + 2(1) - 4 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth these methods work fine, but method $1$ is usually faster.\n\nFinally, note that we can evaluate the difference between two functions using the same methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "74671b66-6f9b-59a5-96e2-aeb7ca503afe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2834 :tutorial/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c0ba47b0-a276-56f7-965d-acc1c060fc97" :step/math-academy-id 15088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055551}}] :activity/title "The Arithmetic of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054889}]}
